---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ExtLink from '../components/ExtLink.astro';
---

<Base
  title="How it runs"
  description="The hosting pattern every tool on ak3ph.si follows, and the checklist for adding a new one."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1>How it runs</h1>
    <p class="muted">
      Every tool on <span class="mono">*.ak3ph.si</span> follows the same pattern, so each one is
      cheap to run, easy to reason about and easy to remove.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="pattern">The pattern</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>One service per tool</strong> on <ExtLink href="https://railway.com">Railway</ExtLink>,
        built from the official Docker image or from a fork's Dockerfile.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One subdomain per tool</strong>, named by what it does rather than what it's called:{' '}
        <span class="mono">draw.ak3ph.si</span>, not <span class="mono">excalidraw.ak3ph.si</span>. If the
        tool behind it changes, the URL doesn't have to.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A resource cap</strong> on every service (0.5 vCPU and 512 MB by default), so one tool
        can't eat the budget of the others.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A volume for any data.</strong> Containers are disposable; anything that must survive a
        redeploy lives on a mounted volume. Tools that keep everything in the browser get no volume.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>DNS on Cloudflare</strong>: a CNAME to Railway plus a TXT record for domain
        verification. Records are proxied, with SSL/TLS set to Full (strict) so traffic is encrypted
        all the way to Railway, which holds its own certificate for each domain. Rocket Loader and Email
        Obfuscation are off, because they rewrite pages the tools serve.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A monitor in Uptime Kuma</strong> for each tool, shown on the public{' '}
        <ExtLink href="https://status.ak3ph.si">status page</ExtLink>. The badges on this site come from
        there.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Forks live in <ExtLink href="https://github.com/ak3ph-labs">ak3ph-labs</ExtLink></strong>{' '}
        and keep the upstream license and credit. A fork is only made when the tool needs changes; otherwise
        the official image runs as-is.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An entry on this site</strong>: one MDX file with the upstream, license, what runs, where
        data lives, and a write-up of what changed and what went wrong.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="checklist">Adding a tool</h2>
    <ol class="checklist">
      <li>
        <strong>Pick it.</strong> Check the license, whether an official image exists, and whether it
        needs changes. No changes means no fork.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fork if needed</strong> into <span class="mono">ak3ph-labs</span>. Keep the LICENSE file
        and upstream credit, and keep the changes small so upstream updates still merge.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Create the service</strong> from the image or the fork, set the resource cap, and mount a
        volume at the tool's data path if it stores anything.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Add the custom domain</strong> <span class="mono">&lt;function&gt;.ak3ph.si</span> on the
        service, then the CNAME and TXT records on Cloudflare (proxied). Wait until the certificate is
        valid and the site loads over HTTPS.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Lock it down.</strong> Create the admin account straight away if the tool has one, and
        turn off public sign-up if it isn't meant to be open.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Add a monitor</strong> in Uptime Kuma, put it on the public status page, and note its id.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Add the entry</strong> as <span class="mono">src/content/entries/&lt;slug&gt;.mdx</span>{' '}
        with <span class="mono">monitorId</span> set. The build fails if a required field is missing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ship it</strong> through a pull request. Once it merges and deploys, check the live page
        and that the badge shows the right status.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="this-site">This site</h2>
    <p>
      ak3ph.si itself is a static <ExtLink href="https://astro.build">Astro</ExtLink> site served by
      Caddy on Railway, under the same pattern. GitHub stats are fetched once at build time, and status
      badges load as images from the status page, so there is no server code and no client-side
      JavaScript. The source is on <ExtLink href="https://github.com/ak3ph-labs/ak3ph.si">GitHub</ExtLink>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-head {
    padding-block: 2.5rem 1.5rem;
  }
  h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
  }
  .page-head p {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
    max-width: 40rem;
  }
  .prose h2::before {
    content: '## ';
    color: var(--accent);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }
  .checklist li::marker {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }
</style>
